## Commands

```bash
project-workspace add-repo /path/to/repository
project-workspace add-infrastructure /path/to/infrastructure
make help
make status
make git-status
make beads-status
make all-status
make doctor
```

`make status` and `make all-status` show Git state, active and ready Beads work,
and workspace health across the root and every repository in `workspace.json`.
The focused `git-status` and `beads-status` targets show one section only. Status
commands are read-only: they do not fetch Git remotes or synchronize independent
Beads stores.

For workspace-specific checks, add an unowned `workspace.mk` file and extend the
combined target, for example `all-status: ci-status deploy-status`. Keep CI,
deployment, runtime, and release behavior outside the generated core.

Both registration commands support `--dry-run`. Keep custom README content outside the
`project-workspace` marker pairs surrounding the generated lists. Use `bd` from this root
for durable cross-project work. `make doctor` runs a bounded, non-mutating Beads health
probe and reports missing tooling separately from an unusable store.

## Tooling requirements

Workspace tooling supports modern Unix-like systems with Python 3.10+, Git 2.28+,
Beads (`bd`), Bash, Make, and filesystem symlinks. Install `project-workspace` through a
user-owned executable symlink to its repository checkout so the script retains access to
its adjacent templates; copying the executable alone is unsupported.

## Portability boundary

`workspace.json` records local relative topology, not repository remotes. Moving this
workspace together with sources at the same relative paths preserves its links. A clone
of this workspace repository alone still needs each source and infrastructure dependency
at the recorded relative target, workspace Beads restored or synced through a supported
Dolt remote or backup, and optional mgit configuration recreated when its installed skill
path changes. Workspace tooling does not clone, discover, relink, or synchronize those
independently owned dependencies.

## Multi-repository Git (optional)

After registering repositories, opt in to the installed `/setup-multirepo-git` wrapper:

```bash
project-workspace configure-mgit --dry-run
project-workspace configure-mgit
```

The command derives the indexed `repos/` service paths, creates `.mgit.conf` and a
relative `scripts/mgit` link to the installed skill, then verifies the root and first
service. A non-empty `SKILLS_DIR` selects one authoritative skill root; otherwise
complete installations are discovered in Codex, Claude, then Pi's `~/.agents/skills`
roots. Later `add-repo` commands update exact managed configuration transactionally
and verify the new service; conflicting or partial mgit state stops registration before
writes. Infrastructure registration leaves mgit unchanged. The command does not edit
agent permissions or policy. Use `make doctor` to distinguish an unconfigured workspace
from a validated mgit setup.
